<body>
<h1>Unit 2: Vectors</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Vectors are the bookkeeping that lets a physical law stay true no matter how you choose to look at the situation. The unit is about representation: when a scalar law becomes a vector law, and which errors in representation produce wrong answers that still look plausible.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>A vector needs both magnitude and direction; two vectors of equal magnitude pointing in different directions are different vectors, unlike scalars.</li><li>Two vectors are equal only when they have identical magnitude and direction, regardless of the path taken to produce them, which is the formal statement of the principle of transmissibility.</li><li>Components are projections onto a chosen orthonormal frame; the frame is free to choose, but the numerical answer changes with the choice even though the physics does not.</li><li>The dot product A.B = AB cos(theta) is a scalar measuring alignment; the cross product magnitude |A x B| = AB sin(theta) is a scalar measuring perpendicularity, and the vector A x B is axial (a pseudovector).</li><li>Displacement, velocity, acceleration, force and momentum are vectors; speed, energy, temperature, mass and power are scalars.</li><li>Vector addition is commutative and associative, so the order in which forces are added cannot change the result - only the arithmetic errors can.</li><li>A vector needs three independent components in three dimensions, so a two-dimensional diagram silently drops one - the component the page cannot show is exactly the one a cross product uses.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Component arithmetic is valid only inside a single orthonormal frame; mixing components taken from different frames produces a number with no meaning.</li><li>The cross product obeys the right-hand rule and is anti-commutative: A x B = -(B x A), so the order of the operands matters even though the magnitudes are equal.</li><li>Two drawn vectors of equal length that look perpendicular on paper are not perpendicular if the page is scaled unevenly; always check magnitude and direction numerically.</li><li>Adding forces that act on different bodies is a category error: Newton's third-law pairs cancel in the total but each acts on something else, so they cannot be merged into one resultant on one object.</li><li>The cross product is defined only in three dimensions, so the planar perpendicular you draw is a construction convention rather than a coordinate-independent operation.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Degrees in a radian</td><td>180/pi = 57.2958</td></tr><tr><td>Resultant of two equal vectors at 120 degrees</td><td>equal to each one</td></tr><tr><td>Components of a unit vector at 30 degrees to the x-axis</td><td>0.866 and 0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Magnitude of the sum of two unit vectors at 90 degrees</td><td>sqrt(2) = 1.414</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does the quantum description of angular momentum look so different from the classical spinning-top picture?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Spin is not a literal rotation of a rigid body: it has no defined axis in the classical sense, yet behaves like an angular momentum with quantum numbers.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How does the quantum state of a spin-1/2 particle relate to the classical picture of a spinning top, if at all?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>The correspondence is suggestive but not exact: quantum angular momentum has no fixed axis, and the 1924 Stern-Gerlach experiment is still the cleanest experimental statement of the mismatch.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Resolve a 30 N force applied at 40 degrees to the horizontal into components, then find the resultant with a second 20 N force at 120 degrees.</li><li>Find the angle between two vectors of equal magnitude whose sum and difference happen to have the same magnitude, and interpret the result.</li><li>Verify a x (b x c) = b(a.c) - c(a.b) with components, then use it to find the rotation axis of a rigid body.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/4-introduction-to-dynamics-newtons-laws-of-motion">OpenStax: College Physics 2e</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/2-1-scalars-and-vectors">OpenStax: Scalars and Vectors</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/2-3-algebra-of-vectors">OpenStax: Algebra of Vectors</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
